Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A drop in productivity growth is hugely detrimental to overall economic growth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A drop in productivity growth is often hugely detrimental to overall economic growth, due to the immense impact that productivity plays in economic growth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Productivity growth is a huge component in overall economic growth, so a weakening of productivity growth may indicates weak economic growth, even in the long term.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Suppose rGDP (index = 100) and trend productivity growth is 3%/yr. If productivity slows to 1.5%/yr for 4 years, then recovers to 3%:</p><ul><li>Old rGDP path after 4 years: $100×1.034≈112.55100\\times1.03^4 \\approx 112.55100×1.034≈112.55$.</li><li>With slowdown: $100×1.0154≈106.14100\\times1.015^4 \\approx 106.14100×1.0154≈106.14$.</li></ul><p>RGDP is ~6% below the pre-slowdown path and stays there unless productivity growth runs above 3% later to catch up.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic growth is not solely caused by productivity growth\">Economic growth is not solely caused by productivity growth</a><ul><li>While productivity growth is important, it is not the only cause of economic growth.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a><ul><li>This is a point of contrast between productivity growth and uncertainty for economic growth.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Baumol&#x27;s Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries\">Baumol's Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries</a><ul><li>This would mean that growth is more and more being driven my labor sectors, though it may be restricted by the Baumol effect.</li></ul></li></ul>","A good work of literature is morally complex":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This describes the value/nature of literature, in that the best literature is good because it is able to explore the moral complexity of its characters.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important when it comes to evaluating the merit of works of literature.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is the case because the real world cannot be abstracted into a simple black/white or good/bad dichotomy. Rather, by representing different perspectives and complex moral situations that forbid easy categorization, a work of literature is representing a more realistic view of society.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a><ul><li>This, for example, explores such themes.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All the King&#x27;s Men\">All the King's Men</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moby-Dick (book)\">Moby-Dick (book)</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Brothers Karamazov\">The Brothers Karamazov</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Stranger\">The Stranger</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","A principle must satisfy generality, universality, publicity, ordering, and finality in order to be a candidate principle in the original position":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In order for a principle to be considered in the original position, there are several conditions that it must meet.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This outlines some of the requirements that principles must abide by in the original position.</p><h4>Conditions</h4><p>There are five conditions that Rawls identifies in order for a principle to be seriously considered in the original position:</p><ol><li>Generality condition: principles must be general and does not refer to specific people or require knowledge of contingent particulars.</li><li>Universality condition: principles must be universally applicable, can’t be self-defeating, and should be judged given ensured compliance to the principles.</li><li>Publicity condition: principles have to be public, since secret principles which people don’t know about can’t be relied upon in society.</li><li>Ordering condition: principles must produce a deterministic ordering of conflicting claims based on morally relevant factors.</li><li>Finality condition: there are no higher principles, other than the principles defined in the original position, which people appeal to.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical\">We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical</a><ul><li>Perhaps at least partly because of the conditions that it adheres to?</li></ul></li></ul>","A strict deference to moral values may have intended negative side effects":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that strictly following some moral code has downsides.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common theme in many works of literature.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>Examples include Oedipus, Hamlet, and Frankenstein.</li><li>A negative example of this would be Odysseus: during his journey back to his homeland after the Trojan war, he repeatedly lies, and as a result, he is able to make it back home safe and sound.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hamlet (play)\">Hamlet (play)</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Frankenstein (book)\">Frankenstein (book)</a></li></ul>","AI has the potential to eliminate information asymmetry in markets":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the accessibility that AI provides, it will allow consumers to gain more information about the products that they purchase. This can help reduce the information asymmetry that currently exist in markets, allowing consumers to make more optimal choices in their purchase and avoid being taken advantage of.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The article estimates that around a quarter of all consumer purchases are of products that suffer from extreme informational asymmetry, meaning that the ability to reduce or eliminate those asymmetries may be incredibly useful.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>How AI could make markets more efficient depends on two factors:</p><ol><li>People have to know how to correctly use AI, since that is the only way for its benefits to manifest.</li><li>How AI is used by retailers or suppliers, which could affect how much of an advantage the consumer gets.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capital&#x27;s share of income will rise in the future due to AI\">Capital's share of income will rise in the future due to AI</a><ul><li>This is a potential for increased inequality, though the thing described in this note should lower inequality.</li></ul></li></ul>","Autonomous vehicles will lead to a decentralization of cities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Autonomous vehicles (AVs), like all previous technological innovations that have made transportation more efficient, will lead to a further decentralization of cities.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>While some people say that AVs will lead to further centralization of cities, this note argues the opposite.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Some have pointed to the historical occurrence whereby while cars decentralized urban areas, trains served to centralized urban areas. This argument points to the fact that this fact is complicated:</p><ul><li>The railway centralization supposedly occurred during the 19th century, when railroads were constructed in greater numbers, but the reason that centralization increased is that the people who were brought in to work in factors did not earn enough to live outside the city, while those who did earn enough left behind living in the city.</li></ul><p>Another assumption that is made in the claim that AVs will increase centralization is that the entertainment centers are concentrated in the city center, which, while it remains true for some older American cities, is increasingly less the case.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Decentralized systems develop better; centralized systems are more easily maintained\">Decentralized systems develop better; centralized systems are more easily maintained</a><ul><li>Decentralized cities might develop better than centralized ones? Although the type of centralized that is referred to here is geographic, and not political.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Train networks can produce economic value in certain cities\">Train networks can produce economic value in certain cities</a><ul><li>AVs would not do the same.</li></ul></li></ul>","Cat-walk (painting)":"<h4>Notes</h4><p>Its quite interesting how the industrial forms are able to be simplified down to nothing, just a collection of lines and shapes and colors.</p><ul><li>It shows something about the artificiality of human architecture.</li></ul><p>Additionally, as you look closer at the painting, you notice that aspects of it don't really line up: some areas are missing shadows, or the perspective is slightly off, or lines may lead nowhere.</p><h4>References</h4>","Charges refer to different particles depending on the context":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Depending on the context, “charge” in physics could refer to electrons, protons, or ions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Often, physics problems just mention how “charges move,” but it is not explicitly stated what that entails. Clarifying this detail would aid with solving these problems.</p><h4>Carriers of Charge</h4><ul><li>Electrons are negatively charged particles that usually move in conductors.</li><li>Protons are positively charged particles that do not move (though in some models, they are shown moving).</li><li>Ions are atoms that have lost or gained electrons, so they carry a net charge.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A proton and electron carry equal but opposite elementary charges\">A proton and electron carry equal but opposite elementary charges</a><ul><li>Specifies the magnitude of charge that each of these carriers holds.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a conductor, the charged particles remain on the edges of the object\">In a conductor, the charged particles remain on the edges of the object</a><ul><li>Where the mobile carriers described here actually end up in a conductor.</li></ul></li></ul>","Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Christian morality inherently adopts an attitude which makes it obedient to authority.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents a new perspective on the issue of Christian morality.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is used by J.S. Mill in order to reply to the claim that Christian morality is entirely right and cannot be otherwise. This aspect of Christian morality which he identifies as an counterargument to that claim.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion\">Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion</a><ul><li>Perhaps this feature of Christian morality is not a “bug” but a feature?</li></ul></li></ul>","Classical utilitarianism is too demanding":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls argues that classical utilitarianism is only possible in a world where people are perfectly altruistic, since only in such a world would people accept some loss to themselves (especially major disutilities) in order that some other, unknown person may gain even just a marginally greater amount of utility. In short, it is too demanding of people.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of Rawls’ objection towards classical utilitarianism, because the assumption of perfect altruism is clearly unreasonable, especially in Rawls’ framework of rational agents.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Another manifestation of the issue here comes in the form of population ethics. Utilitarians would likely too heavily discount the present in order to increase overall utility, and try to mitigate this through the introduction of a morally irrelevant time preference.</p><ul><li>In the first case, it is too morally demanding of the current generation. But to solve this, a morally irrelevant factor must be introduced to make a nonsensical claim: that of pure time preference.</li></ul><h4>Mill</h4><p>Mill counters this argument by saying that, in general, we only have to attend to private or communal utility, and that someone acting as a general benefactor to a huge number of people is going above and beyond. Utilitarianism is therefore not actually that demanding, since all it demands is that we consider the utility of the people around us.</p><h4>Contractarianism</h4><p>A similar counterargument is presented by Scanlon through his <em>Reasonable Rejection of Extreme Sacrifice</em>, whereby an extreme demand for giving others which would leave us destitute would prevent ourselves from being able to carry out our personal projects.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism\">Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism</a><ul><li>This is part of the reason why this is true.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pure time preference is irrational\">Pure time preference is irrational</a><ul><li>This explains why pure time preference is irrational.</li></ul></li></ul>","Economic conclusions can follow from metaphysical commitments rather than from observation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle’s economic views are built upon the specific metaphysical views that he held, and are not necessarily based on observed/empirical economics.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the nature of his economics, and provides context for the reasons that he makes specific claims.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Based on his metaphysical views of the four causes, the formal cause, efficient cause, material cause, and final cause, things essentially have what he considers a “proper use” which is related to the thing’s final cause. Therefore, Aristotle considers money-making to be excessive greed since money does not have a final cause beyond having the ability to be exchanged for other goods. Therefore, it is improper to merely seek money.</p><h4>References</h4>","Justice in the political sphere corresponds to friendship in the household":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle compares political systems (particularly with respect to their treatment of justice) and analogizes them to situations within a household. In doing so, he finds similarities between the two.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important for understanding how to approach friendships within a household.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li><strong>Monarchy</strong>: <em>father-son</em>; the father rules over the son like a benevolent king, having absolute authority but exercising it for the benefit of the son.</li><li><strong>Aristocracy</strong>: <em>husband-wife</em>; the most natural relationship, where each person focuses on their strengths, where they rule the household based on their respective merit in different tasks.</li><li><strong>Timocracy</strong>: <em>brother-brother</em>; the most equal relationship, the closest to a friendship of virtue found between unrelated peers (since the brothers are roughly equal, have a shared history, and if one is good the other is also likely good since they were raised together).</li></ul><p>The first two are virtue friendships too, but of secondary quality, since they lack equality. Additionally, an implication is that since there are “corruptions” of these forms of government (tyranny, oligarchy, and democracy respectively), the relations in the family can likewise be corrupted. The worse case of such an corruption is tyranny in the household, which is also, in regards to political systems, the worst form of corruption.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics is fundamentally a moral debate, not a factual one\">Politics is fundamentally a moral debate, not a factual one</a><ul><li>Aristotle seems to hold this view, given that he analogizes different forms of the state based on their different ethical approaches to politics.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law\">Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law</a><ul><li>How one engages with others is precisely what friendship is about, so the connection between friendship and politics is natural.</li></ul></li></ul>","One cannot be treated unjustly voluntarily; one also cannot treat oneself unjustly":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is impossible for someone to voluntarily choose to be treated unjustly. To be treated unjustly is a bad thing. And since no one truly wishes for what is bad for them, no one can voluntarily accept injustice. Also, justice must be conducted with more than one person present.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a crucial argument for Aristotle to make, since otherwise, there is a contradictory possibility for someone to be unjust to themselves (for instance, through giving themselves too little when it comes to distributive justice; in such a case the person is gaining something else, perhaps honor).</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Committing suicide, which seems to be an injustice against oneself, is indeed an injustice against the state when viewed through the lens of legal justice. Metaphorically, parts of one’s soul is able to engage unjustly with other parts of the soul, though this is not true “justice” and “injustice” in the sense used elsewhere.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law\">Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law</a><ul><li>There are some specific exceptions to the typical rules of justice, which are outlined here.</li></ul></li></ul>","Politics (book)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A foundational work of political philosophy in which he argues that the state exists by nature and that human beings are “political animals” who achieve their fullest flourishing through life in a community governed by law. He studies different forms of government—monarchy, aristocracy, polity, and their corrupt forms—judging them by whether they serve the common good, and he generally favors a mixed constitution that balances the interests of the many and the few. He also examines the household, citizenship, education, property, and the virtues needed for a stable regime, insisting that politics should aim not merely at power or survival but at creating the conditions for ethical life and human happiness.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>One of the most important and foundational works of political science.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><h5>Nature, Teleology, and the City</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"There is an inherent ontological hierarchy in nature\">There is an inherent ontological hierarchy in nature</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humans are the most ‘political’ animal\">Humans are the most ‘political’ animal</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The city is the fundamental political unit\">The city is the fundamental political unit</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A city is by nature a plurality\">A city is by nature a plurality</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The goal of the city is to foster virtue\">The goal of the city is to foster virtue</a></li></ul><h5>Household, Property, and Acquisition</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic conclusions can follow from metaphysical commitments rather than from observation\">Economic conclusions can follow from metaphysical commitments rather than from observation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Commerce is not ‘natural aquisition’\">Commerce is not ‘natural aquisition’</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Property should be privately held, but commonly used\">Property should be privately held, but commonly used</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property allows for the preservation of specific virtues\">Private property allows for the preservation of specific virtues</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The ills usually blamed on private property come from vice, not from ownership\">The ills usually blamed on private property come from vice, not from ownership</a></li></ul><h5>Justice and Citizenship</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Citizenship is defined by sharing in deliberative and judicial office\">Citizenship is defined by sharing in deliberative and judicial office</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Full political participation requires citizens who are equal in condition, not merely in law\">Full political participation requires citizens who are equal in condition, not merely in law</a></li></ul><h5>Regime Taxonomy and Design</h5><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristotle's Taxonomy of Government</span></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristole's Three Parts of a State</span></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Arguments for kingship must collapse to arguments for aristocracy\">Arguments for kingship must collapse to arguments for aristocracy</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Having a large middle class is beneficial for a society\">Having a large middle class is beneficial for a society</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a democracy, the adjudicative power and the executive power should not be vested in the same person\">In a democracy, the adjudicative power and the executive power should not be vested in the same person</a></li></ul><h5>Rule of Law</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rule of law is preferable to rule by decree\">Rule of law is preferable to rule by decree</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Small transgressions of the rule of law are most dangerous\">Small transgressions of the rule of law are most dangerous</a></li></ul><h5>Regime Preservation and Office-Holding</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A regime should prevent its defining style of organization from being pushed too far\">A regime should prevent its defining style of organization from being pushed too far</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Political leaders should not be able to profit from their offices\">Political leaders should not be able to profit from their offices</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Select into office the person with the greatest amount of the trait required and which is the binding constraint\">Select into office the person with the greatest amount of the trait required and which is the binding constraint</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A tyranny makes its population uneducated, mistrust one another, and incapable\">A tyranny makes its population uneducated, mistrust one another, and incapable</a></li></ul><h5>The Best Regime and Education</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Goods of the soul are most valuable\">Goods of the soul are most valuable</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state should be entirely in charge of education\">The state should be entirely in charge of education</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People should be trained in the arts so they have refined aesthetic palates\">People should be trained in the arts so they have refined aesthetic palates</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Of all the arts, music is most able to improve the character of the soul\">Of all the arts, music is most able to improve the character of the soul</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nicomachean Ethics\">Nicomachean Ethics</a><ul><li>The ethics is the prerequisite for the Politics, and contains discussion of what makes an individual good.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"On Liberty\">On Liberty</a><ul><li>Also discusses the correct role of the state.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Theory of Justice\">A Theory of Justice</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Rectificatory justice concerns undoing a transaction between individuals":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle’s defines rectifictory justice as justice that undos the effects of a transactions (without regard to the relative merit of the parties involved).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is another type of justice that Aristotle defines, and a major type within the class of particular justices.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are further variations of rectifictory justice:</p><ul><li><strong>Voluntary</strong>: when people under dealing of their own volition: sale, loan, fire, deposit, etc. Rectifictory Justice here means cancelling unfair gain and loss. </li><li><strong>Involuntary</strong>: when at least one party is not consenting, since they are being wronged: theft, assault, fraud, etc. In such a case, the involuntary party is not unjust, and only the party perpetrating the involuntary action is unjust.<ul><li><strong>Clandestine</strong>: when wrongs are done covertly: theft, adultery, poisoning, etc.</li><li><strong>Violent</strong>: when wrongs are done openly by force: assault, imprisonment, murder, etc.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Distributive Justice means that each individual receives the amount of something commensurate to their merit\">Distributive Justice means that each individual receives the amount of something commensurate to their merit</a><ul><li>This is another type of particular justice.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"There are many possible ways to hurt someone in a transaction\">There are many possible ways to hurt someone in a transaction</a><ul><li>Rectifictory justice deals with how to rectify bad transactions.</li></ul></li></ul>","Retributive punishment is never justified":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that retribution punishments are never justified, which is often justified by invoking several theses (together):</p><ol><li>determinism (or some form of compatibilism) is true such that we are not at least fully responsible for our actions, </li><li>suffering is never intrinsically good, </li><li>we can punish the crime-doer not directly through retributive punishment, and </li><li>punishment’s main goal should be deterrence and prevention.</li></ol><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a significant perspective on criminal justice that is shared by philosophers such as Scanlon.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Scanlon argues that while we cannot wish for an offender to suffer, we are justified in being indifferent to their misfortune. While theses responses may cause the offender to suffer, we are justified in doing this since the offender has already broken the contract of interpersonal relations (i.e., indifferent to the interests of others).</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Nagel notes that there is an intuitive unappealingness with the fact that we cannot believe in the fact that the wicked should suffer.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free will and responsibility are not mutually exclusive\">Free will and responsibility are not mutually exclusive</a><ul><li>This is one of the parts of the argument presented here.</li></ul></li></ul>","Sincerity means coming to terms with one’s baser side":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that being sincere to oneself requires coming to terms with one’s baser side, accepting that it exists but not letting it overtake you.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important way to ensure that one is able to accept oneself and sees oneself clearly and fairly.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is what father Zossima argues that one ought to do in <em>The Brothers Karamazov.</em> This is the thing that Dimitri attempts to do all throughout the book. One when holds secrets about the dark thoughts that go through their head, it isolates the person from the rest of society.</p><h4>References</h4>","Soft power is premised on hard power":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Soft power is something that is largely built on top of hard power, i.e., a foundation of hard power is needed in order for soft power to succeed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Huntington argues that the soft power exhibited by the East Asian countries are built upon their hard power.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Soft power is based on the attractiveness of culture/ideology, and soft power is only seen as affective because it is rooted in the material success and influence of a region that exhibited the power. The success of the East Asian states make their soft power particular effective.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The West is in decline\">The West is in decline</a><ul><li>The soft (and consequently, hard) power of East Asian is partly the reason for the decline of the West.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security\">The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security</a><ul><li>Perhaps the weakened state of the Western military made it less able to exert soft power over the world.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Glorious Revolution expedited the Industrial Revolution":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Glorious Revolution gave the English parliament, and thereby, the English landowners much more power. This allowed them to pass legislation that directly benefitted them, and also the country as a whole.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains one of the reasons why industrialization occurred in English around this time, but no where else.’</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are several reasons pointed to as how the Glorious Revolution impact English industrialization:</p><ol><li>Fundamentally, the Glorious Revolution increased the legislative efficiency of Parliament, meaning that more legislation can be passed.</li><li>This went into the passage of enclosure acts, which secured/centralized land holdings for landowners, and this meant that there was far less land use inefficiency (this allowed for economies of scale).</li><li>Increased efficiency meant that Parliament was able to take a strategy of dealing in detail with cases of estate laws. Previously, in order to avoided the descends of a family from splitting off the estate (which may cause inefficiency, though this was not the main reason for the passage of these laws), many positive expected value investments were forgone. Now, with increased inefficiency, Parliament was able to engage with these cases more on an individual basis, and thus allow for productivity-improving requests to be granted.<ol><li>Sometimes, these improvements meant improvements to transportation, such as the construction of new ports or canals, and this decrease in transportation costs was also crucial.</li></ol></li><li>Specifically, turnpike trusts were established, which allowed for local landowners and merchants to form a non-profit in order to invest in their nearby roads. This greatly improved the ability for materials to be transferred across the nation, and thus lead to industrialization.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Property rights should not be too powerful\">Property rights should not be too powerful</a><ul><li>They were too powerful before the Parliamentarian intervention and evaluation of individual cases.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Since the Industrial Revolution, inventions have occurred more readily\">Since the Industrial Revolution, inventions have occurred more readily</a><ul><li>Perhaps the increase in Parliament’s efficiency also lead to more patents?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Before the Industrial Revolution, there was a disconnect between propositional knowledge and prescriptive knowledge\">Before the Industrial Revolution, there was a disconnect between propositional knowledge and prescriptive knowledge</a><ul><li>Related to the former?</li></ul></li></ul>","The Hojo clan fell due to resentment from other warrior families, the Mongol invasion, and resentment from the emperor":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Hojo fell from power due to resentment from the other warrior families, as the Hojo held de facto power in Japan as the regents for the Kamakura, the cost of the defense against the Mongols, and the allying of the emperor with enemies of the Hojo.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the fall of the Hojo clan, and the rise of the Ashikaga shogunate.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence\">The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence</a></li></ul>","The Mongols took ideas from the peoples that they conquered":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Just like how The Romans learned from the people they conquered , the Mongols did the same thing, adopting the advanced siege machinery invented by other civilizations and tolerating their ideas regarding civic society and religion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Their tolerance and adaptation is part of the reason that they were so successful in their conquests.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Romans learned from the people they conquered\">The Romans learned from the people they conquered</a></li></ul>","The Practical Syllogism motivates us to perform some action":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Practical Syllogism consists of the major premise (regarding a universal), a minor premise (regarding the particular), and the concluding action.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the tool that Aristotle uses to diagnose action, and why even when people know that they ought to do something, they don’t do that thing.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li><strong>Major premise:</strong> being late to school is bad.</li><li><strong>Minor premise:</strong> I’ve hit the snooze button on my alarm clock twice now, and it is a Monday morning.</li><li><strong>Action:</strong> \\[gets out of bed, dresses, and heads to school].</li></ul><h4>Notes</h4><p>The final conclusion of the syllogism isn’t some knowledge. It is the action itself, the action necessitated by the major and minor premises. Additionally, the syllogism can fail. There are three broad categories when this happens:</p><ol><li><strong>Potential:</strong> we are disabled from being able to our knowledge of the world due to external circumstances (e.g., we are asleep).</li><li><strong>Partial:</strong> we understand the universal principle, but fail to see the specific case that relates to the universal.</li><li><strong>Physiological:</strong> Our passions have physically altered our state (e.g., we are drunk), making us temporarily unable to graph reality.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires\">Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires</a><ul><li>The continent person is able to keep desire from hijacking his usage of the Practical Syllogism by desire.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A variety of factors could cause us to be incontinent, or lack self-control\">A variety of factors could cause us to be incontinent, or lack self-control</a><ul><li>The incontinent man has desire disable his perception of the particular in a circumstance, and thereby leads him down the wrong path.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Licentiousness is acting wrongly voluntarily\">Licentiousness is acting wrongly voluntarily</a> <ul><li>The licentious man’s universal premise is corrupted, and thus, the action resulting from the syllogism is likewise corrupted.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Reformation did not engender the spirit of capitalism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Reformation was not what initially created the spirit of capitalism. Rather, it only contributed to the “qualitative and quantitative” expansion of the spirit across the world. He claims that the Protestant Ethics is only one among many causes of the development of the modern view of capitalism.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Weber stresses the idea that the Reformation did not create the spirit of capitalism, as it evident by the fact that capitalism existed, and even flourished, in many parts of the world before that point.</p><h4>Cultural Determinism</h4><p>Many today argue that Weber’s work is a work that espoused Cultural Determinism, the idea that certain cultures are destined to create certain institutions (in this case, that Protestants are somehow predisposed, as a result of their cultural practices, to create modern capitalism). This is not what Weber attempts to do in his book. He simply claims that Protestantism had an impact in the way that capitalism had been shaped, not that Protestantism is the root of modern capitalism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li></ul>","The Thin Theory of the Good serves as a standard framework for agents to consider in the original position":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Thin Theory of the Good is the framework that is adopted in the original position in order to judge the outcomes of particular arrangements of society.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the framework that is used in the original position in the bargaining.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Agents in the original position can’t know about that particular circumstances of the society that they are, though they need some way of predicting approximately the good that will be considered by the people operating in the society afterward, and therefore, they adopt the most conservative conception of that good, which is the Thin Theory. The Thin Theory of the Good would be in line with the principles of justice since:</p><ol><li>In a well-ordered society, there would be social pressure to do so.</li><li>In such a society, we cannot pinpoint an attack on anybody, meaning that one is disincentivized to act rashly and aggressively towards others, and thereby abide by the principles of justice.</li><li>According to the Aristotelian Principle, people are complementary for each other such that the total capability of people together outweighs people’s individual capabilities. However, in order to maintain the well-ordered society in which this occurs, the principles of justice need to be maintained.</li><li>The Kantian Interpretation states that the desire to act justly and the desire to express our nature as free and independent persons in one in the same. Both principles would be chosen in the original position, and best allow us to express ourselves as rational and equal persons.<ul><li>We can differ in our expression of our nature as free and rational persons, but we cannot weigh the sense of justice against other ends.</li></ul></li></ol><p>The indeterminacy of the Thin Theory is acceptable given its fundamental requirement to be coherent with justice, and given that, any other variations are innocuous.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long term goodness is the means to a happy life\">Long term goodness is the means to a happy life</a><ul><li>In short, this is very similar to Rawls’ matter on the issue as well.</li></ul></li></ul>","The labor market is not perfectly competitive":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There has been a misconception that the labor market is perfectly or near perfectly competitive. However, this does not seem to be the case.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Many policies are based on the assumption that the labor market is near-perfectly competitive, so understanding that it is not can have policy implications.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Many of the effects of monopoly are present in the labor market, which seems to be a monopsony applied to the worker, not the consumer.</li><li>Firm mergers should be judged on whether they actually increase productivity and raise wages, rather than just transfer wealth through firing or wage suppression.</li><li>Downward Wage Pression ($DWP$) can be measured with $Markdown \\times Diversion \\ Ratio$, where if it rises about 1-2%, then it indicates a significant incentive to suppress wages.</li><li>There is labor market power because usually there are only a few employers in a location that require specific skills and there are search frictions for jobs.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Non-compete clauses are bad for the worker and the consumer\">Non-compete clauses are bad for the worker and the consumer</a><ul><li>A concrete instance of the imperfection, and of the harm it does.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The decline in the labor share of capital is related to a decline in construction productivity\">The decline in the labor share of capital is related to a decline in construction productivity</a><ul><li>The assumption of the competitiveness of labor led to policies that, since labor is actually not perfectly competitive, actually harm laborers.</li></ul></li></ul>","The right is not the same as the good":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The right is not the same thing as the good.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of the argument that Rawls uses to refute utilitarianism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are three reasons Rawls identifies why the right is not the same thing as the good:</p><ol><li>The principles of justice (the right) are chosen in the original position, while what an individual considers to be good is not chosen at all, but merely arbitrary.</li><li>It is generally desirable for conceptions of the good to differ, but this is not the case for conceptions of justice.</li><li>The explication of the principles of justice must be conducted from behind the Veil of Ignorance, while goodness requires the full knowledge of particulars in the world.</li></ol><p>All these principles imply a <strong>priority of right</strong> such that what is right overrides claims of what is good. This means that perverse utilitarian outcomes, such as when a majority suppresses the rights of a minority with order to satisfy their own good, cannot occur.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Classical utilitarianism is too demanding\">Classical utilitarianism is too demanding</a><ul><li>If they are not, the priority of rights is required, and thus utilitarianism is disqualified as a valid system of justice.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism\">Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism</a></li></ul>","Train networks can produce economic value in certain cities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Three factors allow for train networks to have positive economic value:</p><ol><li>High urban density,</li><li>High rail density (contributing to network effects),</li><li>Limited car reliance.</li></ol><h4>Significance</h4><p>This demonstrates the principles that can make a rail network successful, and can to applied in analyzing different rail networks.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Bamba calibrates an urban commuting model on Tokyo and finds household welfare gains exceeding construction, operating and maintenance costs together. Three results sharpen the three conditions above.</p><ol><li>The returns are concentrated in the dense core. Subway segments return several times what radial extensions do, and the marginal return on further radial expansion is diminishing and in places negative.</li><li>The gains are a network property rather than a sum of line-level gains. Removing the transfer connections into subway stations eliminates roughly $43\\%$ of them.</li><li>The system is sensitive to both density and the car margin. It remains beneficial at New York densities and becomes unprofitable at Los Angeles densities, and raising car speeds to American levels cuts the gains by about $30\\%$ while pushing activity out of the centre.</li></ol><h4>Note</h4><p>The third result is the one with transferable content: rail value depends on the absence of a competitive alternative as much as on the presence of demand, so the same network can be worth building in one city and not in another with identical construction costs.</p><h4>References</h4>","Understanding, good deliberation, and judgment are needed for the virtue of prudence":"<h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Understanding (<em>synesis</em>) is the ability to judge the statements or decisions of others critically.</li><li>Good Deliberation (<em>euboulia</em>) is the ability to reason well and to a good goal.</li><li>Judgement (<em>gnome</em>) is the ability to see with “fairness,” when particular things in the world are unjust. </li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>These minor intellectual virtues are required in order for someone to be prudent.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Cleverness, or resourcefulness (<em>deinotes</em>) is a neutral faculty that describes the ability of an individual to reason well. It then depends on if they reason to some good (or bad) end that this becomes good deliberation.</p><ul><li>If cleverness is used to do bad, it becomes unscrupulousness.</li><li>If cleverness is used to do good, it becomes good deliberation (and thus prudence).</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue\">The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Older people are more prudent because they are more experienced\">Older people are more prudent because they are more experienced</a><ul><li>Naturally, it seems that older people then are better versed in these minor virtues than younger people.</li></ul></li></ul>","Xi Jinping seeks to establish a revolutionary spirit to maintain ideological purity and prevent institutional decay":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Xi seeks to enact a “self-revolution” (自我革命), essentially an attempt to redefine the party with a revolutionary zeal and to ensure that it does not become stagnant and corrupt. This is the reason for his anti-corruption campaigns.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the fundamental ideological justification for Xi’s anti-corruption campaigns and lays out his grand vision for the party.</p><h4>References</h4>"});
